<!--
    Big-flip per-pane strip Slice 2a (#54): the VISUAL re-skin of the per-leaf
    tab strip. Each row is re-skinned to LOOK like a classic MUX TabViewItem —
    rounded top corners, per-state backgrounds, the connected selected-tab
    background, the TabView foreground/border/padding/height — adapted from the
    WinUI 2.8 TabViewItem ControlTemplate + its TabViewItem* theme brushes
    (packages\Microsoft.UI.Xaml.2.8.4 Generic.xaml +
    dev/TabView/TabView_themeresources.xaml).

    Slice 2a.1/2a.2 (#54) follow-up — match the CLASSIC WT tab strip exactly:
      (1) The selected tab is marked by a CONNECTED BACKGROUND SHAPE (a
          rounded-top fill that merges into the content area below), NOT a
          bottom accent bar. Slice 2a's bottom accent underline was removed.
          Slice 2a.2: that fill is the LIVE TERMINAL BACKGROUND COLOR, projected
          per-tab through PaneTabViewModel.Background (x:Bind in the item
          template) — exactly how the classic WT focused tab is painted
          "tab.background = terminalBackground" (Tab::_RecalculateAndApplyTabColor).
          Slice 2a.1 had bound a STATIC LayerOnMicaBaseAltFillColorDefaultBrush
          here under the false premise that it equals the content surface; it
          does NOT (measured: it floated up at #333333, lighter than the strip,
          vs the classic #0C0C0C that merges down). A static theme brush can
          never match an arbitrary profile's background, so the static
          selected-fill key was removed from the brush block below.
      (2) Light-theme contrast fix: the foreground helper now resolves the
          theme-aware Text* brushes correctly (it had been falling through to a
          white fallback in BOTH themes — correct-by-accident in Dark, faint in
          Light). See PaneTabViewModel::ActiveToForeground.

    CRITICAL — selection is a PURE PROJECTION of the model. The SELECTED chrome
    (connected selected background + selected foreground/weight) is driven
    ENTIRELY by PaneTabViewModel.IsActive (OneWay), NOT by the ListView's
    click-selection / ListViewItem.IsSelected. A user click raises
    RequestActivate -> the page dispatches the model action -> the diff flips
    IsActive -> the chrome re-projects. The DEFAULT ListViewItem selection
    visual is NEUTRALIZED in the ItemContainerStyle (its Selected* states paint
    nothing) so the ONLY "selected" look is the IsActive-driven one in the item
    template. The container's CommonStates (PointerOver / Pressed) carry no
    model meaning and so MAY use the container pointer states.

    OUT OF SCOPE this slice (a later 2b): the "+" new-tab button, hover-reveal
    of the close button (it stays always-visible here), keyboard navigation,
    overflow. Activation/close wiring and the model are unchanged.
-->
<UserControl x:Class="TerminalApp.TabStripView"
             xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"
             xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
             xmlns:d="http://schemas.microsoft.com/expression/blend/2008"
             xmlns:local="using:TerminalApp"
             xmlns:mc="http://schemas.openxmlformats.org/markup-compatibility/2006"
             mc:Ignorable="d">

    <UserControl.Resources>
        <!--
            ====================================================================
            TRANSITIONAL COPY of the MUX TabViewItem* theme brushes — RECONCILE
            BEFORE UPSTREAM PR.
            ====================================================================
            The TabViewItem* / TabViewSelectedItem* keys are defined only inside
            WinUI's dev/TabView/TabView_themeresources.xaml, scoped to the
            TabView control's resources — they are NOT exposed at app level. To
            re-skin a ListViewItem with the classic look we re-declare exactly
            the subset we use here, mapping each key to the SAME base WinUI
            common brush the upstream source maps it to (those base brushes
            *are* app-level via XamlControlsResources in App.xaml). Values copied
            verbatim from microsoft-ui-xaml release/2.8
            dev/TabView/TabView_themeresources.xaml. The per-mode sizing
            constants below it are likewise copied from that same file. This
            duplication is intentional + isolated per the locked decision that
            transitional duplication is acceptable IF obvious + isolated; fold
            it back into a shared dictionary before the upstream PR.
        -->
        <ResourceDictionary>
            <ResourceDictionary.ThemeDictionaries>
                <ResourceDictionary x:Key="Default">
                    <StaticResource x:Key="PaneTabHeaderBackground" ResourceKey="LayerOnMicaBaseAltFillColorTransparentBrush" />
                    <!--
                        Slice 2a.4 follow-up (#54): there is NO static hover/pressed
                        fill key anymore. The hover highlight is now the LIVE CONTENT
                        background color at 0.6 opacity, projected per-tab through
                        PaneTabViewModel.Background (the HoverBackground Border in the
                        item template binds Background @ Opacity 0.6, gated by
                        HoverToVisibility(IsHovered, IsActive)). That matches the
                        authoritative classic-WT hover color (Tab.cpp:
                        hoverTabBrush.Color(color); hoverTabBrush.Opacity(0.6) where
                        `color` is the terminal/tab background) — content-aware, NOT a
                        static black overlay. The old static
                        PaneTabHeaderBackgroundPointerOver / *Pressed keys (Black @0.18 /
                        @0.28) are removed; the container PointerOver VSM that used them
                        desynced the highlight from the separator hide, so it is gone.
                    -->
                    <!--
                        Slice 2a.2 (#54): there is NO static selected-tab fill key
                        anymore. The selected fill is the LIVE terminal background
                        COLOR, projected per-tab through PaneTabViewModel.Background
                        (x:Bind in the item template), the same way the classic WT
                        focused tab is painted "tab.background = terminalBackground".
                        A static theme brush can never match an arbitrary profile's
                        background, so PaneTabHeaderBackgroundSelected was removed.
                    -->
                    <StaticResource x:Key="PaneTabHeaderForeground" ResourceKey="TextFillColorSecondaryBrush" />
                    <StaticResource x:Key="PaneTabHeaderForegroundSelected" ResourceKey="TextFillColorPrimaryBrush" />
                    <StaticResource x:Key="PaneTabSeparator" ResourceKey="DividerStrokeColorDefaultBrush" />
                    <StaticResource x:Key="PaneTabBorderBrush" ResourceKey="CardStrokeColorDefault" />
                </ResourceDictionary>
                <ResourceDictionary x:Key="Light">
                    <StaticResource x:Key="PaneTabHeaderBackground" ResourceKey="LayerOnMicaBaseAltFillColorTransparentBrush" />
                    <!--  Slice 2a.4 follow-up (#54): no static hover/pressed fill key — the hover highlight is the live content bg color @0.6 via PaneTabViewModel.Background (see Default).  -->
                    <!--  Slice 2a.2 (#54): no static selected fill — it's the live content bg color via PaneTabViewModel.Background (see Default).  -->
                    <StaticResource x:Key="PaneTabHeaderForeground" ResourceKey="TextFillColorSecondaryBrush" />
                    <StaticResource x:Key="PaneTabHeaderForegroundSelected" ResourceKey="TextFillColorPrimaryBrush" />
                    <StaticResource x:Key="PaneTabSeparator" ResourceKey="DividerStrokeColorDefaultBrush" />
                    <StaticResource x:Key="PaneTabBorderBrush" ResourceKey="CardStrokeColorDefault" />
                </ResourceDictionary>
                <ResourceDictionary x:Key="HighContrast">
                    <StaticResource x:Key="PaneTabHeaderBackground" ResourceKey="SystemColorButtonFaceColorBrush" />
                    <!--  Slice 2a.4 follow-up (#54): no static hover/pressed fill key — the hover highlight is the live content bg color @0.6 via PaneTabViewModel.Background (see Default). In HC the terminal's own bg tracks the HC scheme, so the hovered tab still reads correctly.  -->
                    <!--
                        Slice 2a.2 (#54): no static selected fill in HighContrast
                        either. The selected tab's fill is the live content
                        background color projected through PaneTabViewModel.Background
                        (see Default) — in HC the terminal's own bg already tracks
                        the HC scheme, so the selected tab still connects to the
                        content. Foreground stays the window-text color for max
                        contrast on it.
                    -->
                    <StaticResource x:Key="PaneTabHeaderForeground" ResourceKey="SystemColorGrayTextColorBrush" />
                    <StaticResource x:Key="PaneTabHeaderForegroundSelected" ResourceKey="SystemColorWindowTextColorBrush" />
                    <StaticResource x:Key="PaneTabSeparator" ResourceKey="SystemColorWindowTextColorBrush" />
                    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="PaneTabBorderBrush" Color="{ThemeResource SystemColorWindowTextColor}" />
                </ResourceDictionary>
            </ResourceDictionary.ThemeDictionaries>

            <!--  Sizing constants — copied verbatim from TabView_themeresources.xaml.  -->
            <x:Double x:Key="PaneTabItemMinHeight">32</x:Double>
            <x:Double x:Key="PaneTabItemHeaderFontSize">12</x:Double>
            <Thickness x:Key="PaneTabItemHeaderPadding">8,3,4,3</Thickness>

            <!--
                Tab corner radius: TOP corners only (the ControlCornerRadius
                value of 4, applied to the top two corners; bottom two SHARP).
                Classic WT tabs are sharp at the bottom and the selected tab's
                fill runs straight down — square-cornered — flush into the
                content below, so it reads as a connected "folder tab" rather
                than a floating rounded pill. A uniform ControlCornerRadius
                rounded the BOTTOM corners too, which detached the selected fill
                from the content (the rounded bottom revealed the strip behind
                each corner). Used by both the selected and pointer backgrounds
                so every tab shape matches.
            -->
            <CornerRadius x:Key="PaneTabItemCornerRadius">4,4,0,0</CornerRadius>

            <!--
                Slice 2a.3 (#54): the manila-folder BOTTOM FLARE size. Classic
                WT's WinUI 2.8 TabViewItem paints its selected fill with
                SelectedBackgroundPath Margin="-4,0,-4,0" (Generic.xaml ~3047) —
                the selected tab's fill flares OUTWARD 4px on each side at the
                bottom, with a concave fillet connecting the tab's vertical side
                down to the baseline (the browser/folder "foot"). A bare
                ListViewItem has no TabViewTemplateSettings.TabGeometry to drive
                that Path, so we reproduce the flare as two 4x4 corner fillets
                (see the SelectedBackground siblings in the item template). 4 to
                match the classic SelectedBackgroundPath margin and the top
                corner radius.
            -->
            <x:Double x:Key="PaneTabItemFlareSize">4</x:Double>

            <!--
                Rest-state ListViewItem container style. This is where the
                classic TabViewItem chrome lives: the rounded-top tab shape, the
                rest / pointer-over / pressed backgrounds, the bottom border
                line, the inter-tab separator, and — critically — the
                NEUTRALIZED default selection visuals. The SELECTED look itself
                is NOT here; it is driven from IsActive in the item template (see
                below), so a ListView click never paints a tab selected before
                the model round-trips.

                Adapted from the WinUI 2.8 TabViewItem ControlTemplate (Generic
                .xaml). The selected-state Path/TabGeometry of the original needs
                the TabView's TemplateSettings (unavailable on a bare
                ListViewItem); we substitute a rounded-top Border for the
                selected background, driven by IsActive in the template.
            -->
            <Style x:Key="PaneTabItemContainerStyle"
                   TargetType="ListViewItem">
                <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="{StaticResource PaneTabItemHeaderFontSize}" />
                <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{ThemeResource PaneTabHeaderForeground}" />
                <Setter Property="Background" Value="Transparent" />
                <Setter Property="HorizontalContentAlignment" Value="Stretch" />
                <Setter Property="VerticalContentAlignment" Value="Stretch" />
                <Setter Property="MinHeight" Value="{StaticResource PaneTabItemMinHeight}" />
                <Setter Property="MinWidth" Value="0" />
                <Setter Property="Padding" Value="0" />
                <Setter Property="Margin" Value="0" />
                <Setter Property="UseSystemFocusVisuals" Value="False" />
                <Setter Property="Template">
                    <Setter.Value>
                        <ControlTemplate TargetType="ListViewItem">
                            <Grid x:Name="LayoutRoot"
                                  MinHeight="{TemplateBinding MinHeight}">
                                <VisualStateManager.VisualStateGroups>
                                    <!--
                                        CommonStates — fully NEUTRALIZED. The hover
                                        highlight no longer lives here.
                                        Slice 2a.4 follow-up (#54): the old PointerOver
                                        / PointerOverSelected / Pressed / PressedSelected
                                        setters that filled a container-level
                                        PointerBackground with the static black-overlay
                                        brush are REMOVED — that container PointerOver VSM
                                        fired on a different pointer mechanism than the
                                        page-driven separator hide, so the highlight and
                                        the separators desynced ("a step too slow"). The
                                        hover highlight now lives in the item DataTemplate
                                        (HoverBackground, bound to IsHovered) and is set in
                                        the SAME _recomputePaneTabSeparators pass as the
                                        separators, so they update in lockstep. The
                                        Selected* states stay neutralized for the same
                                        reason they always were: ListView single-select
                                        must not paint its own accent — the ONLY selected
                                        look is the IsActive-driven chrome in the template.
                                    -->
                                    <VisualStateGroup x:Name="CommonStates">
                                        <VisualState x:Name="Normal" />
                                        <VisualState x:Name="PointerOver" />
                                        <VisualState x:Name="Pressed" />
                                        <VisualState x:Name="Selected" />
                                        <VisualState x:Name="PointerOverSelected" />
                                        <VisualState x:Name="PressedSelected" />
                                    </VisualStateGroup>
                                </VisualStateManager.VisualStateGroups>

                                <!--
                                    Bottom border line under non-selected tabs — the
                                    TabViewItem's BottomBorderLine. Stays under every
                                    tab; the selected tab's own rounded background
                                    (in the template) sits on top of it.
                                -->
                                <Border x:Name="BottomBorderLine"
                                        Height="1"
                                        VerticalAlignment="Bottom"
                                        BorderBrush="{ThemeResource PaneTabBorderBrush}"
                                        BorderThickness="1" />

                                <!--  The item content (the IsActive- / IsHovered-driven tab chrome + label + close).  -->
                                <ContentPresenter x:Name="ContentPresenter"
                                                  Padding="0"
                                                  HorizontalContentAlignment="Stretch"
                                                  VerticalContentAlignment="Stretch" />

                                <!--
                                    Slice 2a.3 follow-up (#54): the inter-tab
                                    separator used to live HERE as a container-level
                                    Border drawn on EVERY tab's right edge (faded
                                    only on hover), which left a stray trailing line
                                    after the selected/only tab. It is now a MODEL
                                    PROJECTION moved into the item DataTemplate (so
                                    it can x:Bind the VM's ShowSeparator) and shown
                                    ONLY between two consecutive UNSELECTED tabs
                                    (classic WT). The hover-fade of the separator is
                                    dropped here (acceptable — a Slice 2b polish).
                                -->
                            </Grid>
                        </ControlTemplate>
                    </Setter.Value>
                </Setter>
            </Style>
        </ResourceDictionary>
    </UserControl.Resources>

    <ListView x:Name="TabsList"
              Background="{ThemeResource TabViewBackground}"
              ItemContainerStyle="{StaticResource PaneTabItemContainerStyle}"
              ScrollViewer.HorizontalScrollBarVisibility="Auto"
              ScrollViewer.HorizontalScrollMode="Auto"
              ScrollViewer.VerticalScrollBarVisibility="Disabled"
              ScrollViewer.VerticalScrollMode="Disabled"
              SelectionMode="Single">
        <ListView.ItemsPanel>
            <ItemsPanelTemplate>
                <StackPanel Orientation="Horizontal" />
            </ItemsPanelTemplate>
        </ListView.ItemsPanel>
        <ListView.ItemTemplate>
            <DataTemplate x:DataType="local:PaneTabViewModel">
                <!--
                    Per-tab chrome. The SELECTED look (connected selected
                    background + selected foreground/weight) is driven ENTIRELY
                    by IsActive (OneWay) via the converter-free x:Bind helpers on
                    PaneTabViewModel — a pure projection of the model, never the
                    ListView's click-selection. A tap raises RequestActivate.
                -->
                <!--
                    Slice 2a.4 (#54): PointerEntered/Exited raise the VM's hover
                    INTENT (RequestHoverEnter/Exit), mirroring how Tapped binds to
                    RequestActivate. The page tracks the hovered tab id (pure view
                    state, NEVER written to WorkspaceModel) and recomputes
                    ShowSeparator so hovering a tab hides the dividers on BOTH sides
                    of it — classic WinUI TabViewItem PointerOver sets
                    TabSeparator.Opacity = 0. Bound function-call form (no args), like
                    RequestActivate, so the PointerRoutedEventArgs is ignored.
                -->
                <Grid MinHeight="{StaticResource PaneTabItemMinHeight}"
                      PointerEntered="{x:Bind RequestHoverEnter}"
                      PointerExited="{x:Bind RequestHoverExit}"
                      Tapped="{x:Bind RequestActivate}">
                    <!--
                        Slice 2a.1/2a.2 (#54): the CONNECTED selected-tab
                        background — the rounded-top tab fill shown only when this
                        VM IsActive. This IS the selected cue (the classic
                        TabViewItem look); there is NO separate bottom accent bar.
                        Slice 2a.2: its fill is the LIVE TERMINAL BACKGROUND COLOR,
                        projected through PaneTabViewModel.Background (a fresh
                        non-acrylic SolidColorBrush the page extracts from the
                        mounted content's BackgroundBrush(), refreshed via the
                        title-cadence subscription). This is the faithful classic
                        WT behavior — the focused tab is painted with
                        "tab.background = terminalBackground"
                        (Tab::_RecalculateAndApplyTabColor) — so the selected tab
                        reads as the SAME surface as the content area below and
                        merges DOWN into it. (Slice 2a.1 wrongly bound a STATIC
                        LayerOnMicaBaseAltFillColorDefaultBrush here under the false
                        premise that it equals the content surface; it does not —
                        it floated UP, lighter than the strip. That static key is
                        now dead for the selected fill.) The Border extends to the
                        very bottom of the row (only a 1px top inset for the
                        rounded top) so it OVERPAINTS the container template's
                        BottomBorderLine — the selected tab has no border line
                        beneath it. Its corners are rounded on TOP only
                        (PaneTabItemCornerRadius) and SQUARE at the bottom, so the
                        fill runs straight down flush into the content and reads as
                        a connected folder tab (classic), not a floating rounded
                        pill. Substitutes the TabViewItem's SelectedBackgroundPath
                        (whose TabGeometry needs the TabView TemplateSettings we
                        don't have on a bare ListViewItem).
                        Pre-mount the Background binding is null (the tab not yet
                        mounted) and the Border renders transparent/neutral until
                        ContentMounted seeds it — immediate in practice for the
                        active tab, and NOT the old inverted floating look.
                        x:Name'd so headless TAEF can assert its presence +
                        IsActive-driven visibility without a layout pass.
                    -->
                    <Border x:Name="SelectedBackground"
                            Margin="0,1,0,0"
                            Background="{x:Bind Background, Mode=OneWay}"
                            CornerRadius="{StaticResource PaneTabItemCornerRadius}"
                            Visibility="{x:Bind local:PaneTabViewModel.ActiveToVisibility(IsActive), Mode=OneWay}" />

                    <!--
                        Slice 2a.4 follow-up (#54): the HOVER HIGHLIGHT. A sibling of
                        SelectedBackground (same layer position, same rounded-top /
                        square-bottom tab shape), shown ONLY on a hovered UNSELECTED
                        tab via HoverToVisibility(IsHovered, IsActive) — so it and
                        SelectedBackground are mutually exclusive (the selected tab
                        keeps its connected look, never both). Its fill is the LIVE
                        CONTENT background color at 0.6 opacity (PaneTabViewModel
                        .Background @ Opacity 0.6), which is the AUTHORITATIVE classic-WT
                        hover color: Tab.cpp paints hoverTabBrush.Color(color);
                        hoverTabBrush.Opacity(0.6) where `color` is the terminal/tab
                        background. This REPLACES the old static Black @0.18 overlay
                        that the container ControlTemplate's PointerOver VSM filled —
                        that VSM is removed so the highlight (this IsHovered-bound
                        Border, set in the SAME _recomputePaneTabSeparators pass as the
                        separator hide) and the separators update in lockstep, never
                        desynced. IsHovered is PURE VIEW STATE, never written to the
                        WorkspaceModel.
                    -->
                    <Border x:Name="HoverBackground"
                            Margin="0,1,0,0"
                            Background="{x:Bind Background, Mode=OneWay}"
                            CornerRadius="{StaticResource PaneTabItemCornerRadius}"
                            Opacity="0.6"
                            Visibility="{x:Bind local:PaneTabViewModel.HoverToVisibility(IsHovered, IsActive), Mode=OneWay}" />

                    <!--
                        Slice 2a.3 (#54) + follow-up: the manila-folder BOTTOM
                        FLARE, a CONCAVE chamfer foot. The SelectedBackground
                        border above is the tab BODY — rounded TOP (4,4,0,0),
                        SQUARE bottom, full cell width. These two 4x4
                        corner-fillet Paths add the OUTWARD flare at the two
                        BOTTOM corners so the selected tab matches classic WT
                        exactly: classic's WinUI 2.8 TabViewItem selected fill is
                        SelectedBackgroundPath with Margin="-4,0,-4,0"
                        (Generic.xaml ~3047) — the fill flares 4px outward each
                        side at the bottom with a CONCAVE fillet hugging the
                        baseline (the browser/folder foot), the dark boundary
                        curving TOWARD the tab body. A bare ListViewItem has no
                        TabViewTemplateSettings.TabGeometry to drive that Path, so
                        we substitute two corner fillets here. Each:
                          * Fill = the LIVE content background color (the SAME
                            PaneTabViewModel.Background brush the body uses), so
                            the foot MERGES SEAMLESSLY into both the body and the
                            content area below — exactly how classic paints
                            "tab.background = terminalBackground".
                          * IsActive-gated via ActiveToVisibility(IsActive) — the
                            identical pure-model projection the body uses, so ONLY
                            the selected tab flares; this is NEVER the ListView
                            click-selection.
                          * VerticalAlignment=Bottom, 4x4, pinned to the bottom
                            corner and extended FlareSize px outward beyond the
                            cell (left flare Margin="-4,0,0,0", right
                            Margin="0,0,-4,0").
                        Geometry is a CONCAVE quarter-disk fillet (Slice 2a.3
                        follow-up: was a convex bump bulging OUTWARD into the
                        strip; the arc-sweep flag is now flipped so the foot is
                        concave). For the LEFT foot the 4x4 box's right edge (x=4)
                        abuts the tab body's bottom-left corner; the box extends
                        4px to the LEFT (Margin -4) so x=0 is the baseline's
                        outermost point. The path "M4,0 A4,4 0 0 1 0,4 L4,4 Z"
                        traces: tab-body bottom corner (4,0) -> arc (sweep 1,
                        centre at the box's top-left (0,0)) down-and-out to the
                        baseline (0,4) -> line to (4,4) -> close. The filled
                        region is the quarter-disk in the bottom-RIGHT of the box,
                        so the dark area is FULL-width at the baseline and tapers
                        to zero at the top, the arc boundary curving concavely
                        toward the tab — the foot WIDENS the tab ~4px outward at
                        the baseline (classic's concave foot), not a rounded-in
                        pill or an outward convex bump. The right foot mirrors it
                        (sweep 0). Part of the transitional re-skin (fold back
                        into the shared TabViewItem dictionary before the upstream
                        PR).
                    -->
                    <Path HorizontalAlignment="Left"
                          VerticalAlignment="Bottom"
                          Width="{StaticResource PaneTabItemFlareSize}"
                          Height="{StaticResource PaneTabItemFlareSize}"
                          Margin="-4,0,0,0"
                          Data="M4,0 A4,4 0 0 1 0,4 L4,4 Z"
                          Fill="{x:Bind Background, Mode=OneWay}"
                          Visibility="{x:Bind local:PaneTabViewModel.ActiveToVisibility(IsActive), Mode=OneWay}" />
                    <Path HorizontalAlignment="Right"
                          VerticalAlignment="Bottom"
                          Width="{StaticResource PaneTabItemFlareSize}"
                          Height="{StaticResource PaneTabItemFlareSize}"
                          Margin="0,0,-4,0"
                          Data="M0,0 A4,4 0 0 0 4,4 L0,4 Z"
                          Fill="{x:Bind Background, Mode=OneWay}"
                          Visibility="{x:Bind local:PaneTabViewModel.ActiveToVisibility(IsActive), Mode=OneWay}" />

                    <Grid Padding="{StaticResource PaneTabItemHeaderPadding}"
                          ColumnSpacing="6">
                        <Grid.ColumnDefinitions>
                            <ColumnDefinition Width="*" />
                            <ColumnDefinition Width="Auto" />
                        </Grid.ColumnDefinitions>
                        <!--
                            Slice 2a.1 (#54): theme-CORRECT foreground. The label is
                            TWO overlaid TextBlocks, each carrying a theme-aware
                            {ThemeResource} foreground brush so the FRAMEWORK
                            resolves the color per the strip's effective theme —
                            dark on Light, light on Dark. Exactly one shows at a
                            time, gated by IsActive (a pure projection of the
                            model, never click-selection): the SELECTED label
                            (primary brush, bold) when active; the REST label
                            (secondary brush, normal) otherwise. This replaces the
                            Slice-2a imperative ActiveToForeground brush, which
                            snapshotted the APPLICATION theme — WT does not switch
                            it for "theme":"light" — and so painted light-grey text
                            on the Light strip. (x:Bind cannot pass a template
                            element to a helper here — the XAML codegen breaks — so
                            the per-theme brush must come from {ThemeResource}, not
                            an imperative lookup.)
                        -->
                        <TextBlock Grid.Column="0"
                                   VerticalAlignment="Center"
                                   FontSize="{StaticResource PaneTabItemHeaderFontSize}"
                                   FontWeight="{x:Bind local:PaneTabViewModel.ActiveToFontWeight(IsActive), Mode=OneWay}"
                                   Foreground="{ThemeResource PaneTabHeaderForegroundSelected}"
                                   Text="{x:Bind Title, Mode=OneWay}"
                                   TextTrimming="CharacterEllipsis"
                                   Visibility="{x:Bind local:PaneTabViewModel.ActiveToVisibility(IsActive), Mode=OneWay}" />
                        <TextBlock Grid.Column="0"
                                   VerticalAlignment="Center"
                                   FontSize="{StaticResource PaneTabItemHeaderFontSize}"
                                   FontWeight="{x:Bind local:PaneTabViewModel.ActiveToFontWeight(IsActive), Mode=OneWay}"
                                   Foreground="{ThemeResource PaneTabHeaderForeground}"
                                   Text="{x:Bind Title, Mode=OneWay}"
                                   TextTrimming="CharacterEllipsis"
                                   Visibility="{x:Bind local:PaneTabViewModel.RestToVisibility(IsActive), Mode=OneWay}" />
                        <Button Grid.Column="1"
                                Width="20"
                                Height="20"
                                Padding="0"
                                VerticalAlignment="Center"
                                Background="Transparent"
                                BorderThickness="0"
                                Click="{x:Bind RequestClose}">
                            <FontIcon FontFamily="{ThemeResource SymbolThemeFontFamily}"
                                      FontSize="10"
                                      Glyph="&#xE711;" />
                        </Button>
                    </Grid>

                    <!--
                        Slice 2a.3 follow-up (#54): the inter-tab separator, now a
                        MODEL PROJECTION. Moved out of the container ControlTemplate
                        (where it was drawn on every tab and left a stray trailing
                        line) INTO the item DataTemplate so it can x:Bind the VM's
                        ShowSeparator — Visible ONLY when this tab is NOT the last,
                        NOT selected, and its right-neighbour is NOT selected (i.e.
                        only between two consecutive UNSELECTED tabs, classic WT).
                        ShowSeparator is computed by
                        TerminalPage::_recomputePaneTabSeparators after every strip
                        mutation. Right-aligned 1px divider, same Margin/brush as the
                        old container-level Border. The old hover-fade is dropped
                        (Slice 2b polish).
                    -->
                    <Border Width="1"
                            Margin="0,8,0,8"
                            HorizontalAlignment="Right"
                            BorderBrush="{ThemeResource PaneTabSeparator}"
                            BorderThickness="1"
                            Visibility="{x:Bind local:PaneTabViewModel.ShowSeparatorToVisibility(ShowSeparator), Mode=OneWay}" />
                </Grid>
            </DataTemplate>
        </ListView.ItemTemplate>
    </ListView>

</UserControl>
